<section class="section">
  <h2>What it does</h2>
  <p class="section-intro">
    You hand it a <code>GraphQLSchema</code> — or a string of SDL — and it hands
    back a pool of mock objects per type, with relationship fields wired as real
    object references. <code>todo.user</code> is the same object as
    <code>mocks.User[i]</code>, not a copy of it and not a fresh random user.
    Because the data is a graph rather than a pile of unrelated fixtures, an
    arbitrary query can be answered from it: you write the query your component
    already writes, and the selection set decides what comes back.
  </p>

  <ul class="card-grid">
    <li>
      <article class="card">
        <h3>A graph, not fixtures</h3>
        <p>
          Relationships are honoured in both directions and cycles resolve to
          objects that already exist, so back-references point home instead of
          generating forever. Sizes are yours: <code>count</code> per type,
          <code>relations</code> per field, <code>listSize</code> for lists of
          scalars.
        </p>
      </article>
    </li>
    <li>
      <article class="card">
        <h3>Answers any operation</h3>
        <p>
          <code>dataForOperation(doc)</code> runs a document against the graph
          and returns data shaped to its selection set — fragments, unions and
          interfaces included. <code>toRequestHandler()</code> turns the pool
          into one callable that answers every operation, memoizes per document
          and records the calls it saw.
        </p>
      </article>
    </li>
    <li>
      <article class="card">
        <h3>Reproducible, or deliberately awful</h3>
        <p>
          A <code>seed</code> makes the whole build reproducible, down to the
          draws your own overrides make, so a snapshot test is not a coin flip.
          QA presets swap in the data that actually breaks layouts — long text,
          unicode, empty lists, mixed nulls — and scenarios name a combination
          so you can pass it around.
        </p>
      </article>
    </li>
    <li>
      <article class="card">
        <h3>Apollo and Storybook</h3>
        <p>
          <code>createMockClient</code> and <code>mockLink</code> put the graph
          behind a real Apollo client; <code>mockOperation</code> builds
          <code>MockedProvider</code> mocks that resolve from it. The
          <code>withGraphqlMocks</code> decorator gives every story a client and
          a parameter for picking loading, error or QA states.
        </p>
      </article>
    </li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section class="section" id="start">
  <h2>Get started</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <pre class="code"><code>npm install --save-dev @vantreeseba/graphql-mocks graphql @faker-js/faker</code></pre>
    <pre class="code"><code>import { buildMocks } from '@vantreeseba/graphql-mocks';

const mocks = buildMocks(schema, { seed: 42, count: 10, stableIds: true });

mocks.User;                            // 10 User objects — and mocks.Todo[0].user is
                                       // one of them: the same object, not a copy
mocks.byId('User', 'User-2');          // readable ids, thanks to stableIds
mocks.dataForOperation(UserByIdQuery); // answered from the graph, shaped to the
                                       // selection set</code></pre>
    <p>
      That is the whole first step. From there:
      <code>buildMocks(schema, { qa: 'longText' })</code> for the data that
      breaks things, <code>mocks.toRequestHandler()</code> for a transport that
      answers every operation, and the companion codegen plugin
      <code>@vantreeseba/graphql-mocks-codegen</code> for pools that come back
      typed without a cast.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

